========================================================================================================================
L25 -- the universal external field: does the eta-matching force one, and would galaxies survive it?
========================================================================================================================

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART A -- the eta transition, varied rather than assumed at either endpoint
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  E1 -- WHAT eta IS A FUNCTION OF.  This decides the whole question, because it decides WHERE
        the two branches can meet.
  [info] IC5_ACTION.md: activation argument r = -N p/(3 m h0)   found verbatim
  [info] IC5_ACTION.md: p is the TRACE of the metric momentum   found verbatim
  [info] IC5_ACTION.md: switch shape d(r) = (r^2-1)^2   found verbatim
  [info] IC5_ACTION.md: static plateau eta = 0 for |r^2-1| >= 1/2   found verbatim
        eta = eta(r) with r = -N p/(3 m h0) and p the TRACE of the metric momentum: eta is a
        function of the LOCAL EXPANSION RATE alone.  No Phi, no |a|, no rho, no position.
        static side  0 <= r <= 0.707107 (r^2 <= 1/2): max|eta| = 0.000e+00, max|eta'| = 0.000e+00, max|eta''| = 0.000e+00
        cosmological plateau 0.8660 <= r <= 1.1180 (|r^2-1| <= 1/4): max|1-eta| = 0.000e+00
  [PASS] E1 eta's activation variable is the trace metric momentum (the local expansion rate) and nothing else, and its static plateau is EXACT -- eta and all its derivatives vanish identically for r^2 <= 1/2, so a quasi-static region sits at eta = 0 on a NEIGHBOURHOOD, not at a point   (verified against the published definition and by evaluating the switch)

  E2 -- DOES u CARRY A BOUNDARY CONDITION ON THE eta = 0 BRANCH?  If its static Euler-Lagrange
        equation has no derivative of u, then u is algebraically slaved pointwise and NO far-field
        value can be imposed on it at all.
        conformal identity residual  e^(-2w) Rbar - [R3 + 4 Lap_h w - 2|Dw|^2] = 0
  [PASS] E2a the conformal 3-curvature identity the IC5 -> IC1 static transfer relies on is correct, derived here for a general spherically symmetric leaf rather than quoted   (exact zero in sympy, hbar = e^(-2w) h)
        EL residuals of [IC5 static density - IC1 static density] in (Phi, Psi, u): (0, 0, 0)
  [PASS] E2b IC5's static gradient block combines with the barred-curvature difference into an EXACT null Lagrangian, so the eta = 0 sector is IC1's -- independently re-derived here, not inherited   (all three Euler-Lagrange residuals vanish identically)
        dL/du'  = 0,   dL/du'' = 0
        the u equation, divided by 2 m N sqrt(h) u:  -AA + a0**2*log(1 - U0**2)**2 = 0
        substituting a.a = a0^2 ln^2(1-u^2):        0
  [PASS] E2c on the eta = 0 branch u appears in the static Lagrangian with NO derivatives, so its Euler-Lagrange equation is ALGEBRAIC (u^2 = 1 - e^(-|a|/a0) pointwise) and u CANNOT carry a far-field boundary condition of any kind   (no u', no u'': a field with no derivatives in its own EL equation admits no boundary datum)

        => PART 1's structural answer.  Inside eta = 0 the far-field u for an isolated galaxy is
           NEITHER 0-by-choice NOR the cosmological value: it is the pointwise function of the
           galaxy's OWN field, u^2 = 1 - e^(-|a|/a0), which tends to 0 as |a| -> 0.  There is no
           boundary condition to impose, so the cosmological value can only reach a galaxy where
           the region itself leaves eta = 0.  That relocates the question to E3.

  E3 -- WHERE THE eta BOUNDARY SITS around real SPARC galaxies.
        calibrating r against the lead's own solutions -- IC11_CLOCK_PRESSURE's table gives
        r/H_physical = 0.9813, 1.0363, 1.2057, 1.3498  (m = h0 = 1 units)
        taking the LARGEST ratio 1.3498 (conservative: it makes eta leave 0 EARLIEST),
        eta = 0 is guaranteed wherever  H_local <= 0.5239 h0.
        SPARC: 141 galaxies, 2734 points (Upsilon_d = 0.5, Upsilon_b = 0.7, eV/V < 0.10)
        R_last                             : min    1.08  median   13.37  max   108.31  kpc
        R(Hubble-velocity crossing)/R_last : min    31.6  median   117.9  max    550.9
        R(Lambda zero-gravity)/R_last      : min    38.1  median   135.1  max    552.2
        Direction stated: this is a KINEMATIC estimate of the local expansion rate around a bound
        object, NOT a solution of the IC equations in the transition region.  See E4.
  [PASS] E3 the eta boundary around every SPARC galaxy lies at least 10x beyond its last measured radius, so the whole rotation-curve region sits inside the exact eta = 0 plateau on BOTH criteria   (worst case over 141 galaxies and two criteria: 31.6x beyond R_last)

  E4 -- WHAT THE PUBLISHED eta-TRANSITION WORK NOW GIVES, and the one input still missing.
        IC11_TRANSITION.md HAS now varied the transition (IC10 'What remains' item 1): YES  -- L11's B5 wrote it as not varied; that is now superseded.
        BUT on a HOMOGENEOUS background only: a 501-point continuation in the trace momentum rho
        from the IC10 plateau through the switch boundary, giving u = 0.622175 (n=200), 0.625545
        (n=220), 0.666132 (n=500).  Those are the COSMOLOGICAL u values continued in TIME; they are
        not a galactic far field, and no radius appears anywhere in that calculation.
        AND that trial FAILS a necessary scalar kinetic condition, aUV < 0 from n = 205 onward: recorded in the report.
        IC11_CLOCK_PRESSURE.md's own closing liability names the same gap: present.
        THE MISSING INPUT, named exactly: a solution of the full phase equations for a BOUND MASS
        embedded in the expanding plateau, with eta varying in SPACE (not in the homogeneous
        momentum), so that r(x)^2 crosses 1/2 at some radius and u is continued across it.  Nothing
        published attempts that, and the only transition action that HAS been varied is unhealthy.
  [FAIL] E4 the eta-transition, as published, DETERMINES the far-field u for a galaxy embedded in the cosmological solution   (IC11 varied it homogeneously only, and that trial fails a necessary scalar kinetic condition (aUV < 0 from n = 205); no spatially inhomogeneous eta profile around a bound mass exists)

        PART 1's ANSWER, in one line: the far-field u for an isolated galaxy tends to 0 -- NOT the
        cosmological value and NOT something in between -- throughout the eta = 0 region, because u
        is algebraically slaved there and admits no boundary datum (E2); and the eta = 0 region
        extends at least 32x beyond the last measured rotation-curve point (E3).  What is NOT
        settled is whether the full spatial transition leaves that intact (E4).  PART B bounds the
        damage if it does not.

